        CH3−C(+)=O↔CH3−C≡O(+)
The positive charge is delocalized between carbon and oxygen.
✅ Highly resonance-stabilized.
(d) CH₂⁺–CH=O    This carbocation is extremely unstable.
Although it is adjacent to a carbonyl group, the carbonyl is electron-withdrawing (−M and −I effects). The positive charge cannot be effectively stabilized; instead, conjugation with the carbonyl increases electron deficiency. A resonance form is:
        CH2(+)−CH=O↔CH2​=CH−O(+)
This places a positive charge on oxygen while leaving an electron-deficient system. Overall, the carbonyl group destabilizes the adjacent carbocation.

